  "notes": "\"Harmonically closer to church than to trap\" is the entry's own summary and it drives this model. The stated harmony — \"4-chord gospel cycles (`i–iv–VII–III`, minor-line-cliché descents), diminished approach chords, and a whole-step modulation for the last chorus\" — is authored as that family at top weight, a descending minor family beneath it, and the highest `susOrDimProb` in the section for the diminished approach. The lead instruments are unusual for the lane and the entry says so: \"acoustic piano and organ as the lead instruments (unusual in this lane)\", which is the voicing register and the `timbreHint`. The kit is the collision the entry names — a trap kit under a gospel piano bed with \"tambourine on the backbeat, snare rolls into the hook, and a full stop-time break before the final chorus\" — so the tambourine mirrors the clap, the snare-roll ladder is authored, and the arrangement's `dropOutBeats` is high. The vocal is \"full-voiced alto with a large belt and heavy melisma — very high note-density, long runs, cracked-voice ornament\", and \"the lyric is narrative and verse-heavy\", which is the 16-to-24-bar verse. ⚠ `confidence` is **low**, mirroring medium-low: \"the gospel-narrative positioning is sourced; tempo/drum values are inference\". ⚠ All drum, swing and 808 numbers are **INFERRED**. ⚠ `session.bpm.mode` is **INFERRED**: the number is not stated in the entry, and it is an authored centre of gravity inside the band the entry does state. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
